sections.content:
Combining multiple conditions with $and (often implicit when conditions are simply listed together, but explicit $and is needed for combining multiple conditions on the SAME field):

db.products.find({
  $and: [
    { price: { $gte: 10 } },
    { price: { $lte: 100 } }
  ]
});

Querying a nested field directly, using dot notation (extending Module 8, lesson 2's embedded-document pattern):

db.orders.find({ 'shippingAddress.city': 'Austin' });

Querying an array field for a specific contained value:

db.products.find({ tags: 'award-winning' }); // matches if 'award-winning'
                                                  // is ANY element in the array

Querying an array using $elemMatch when multiple conditions must ALL match
the SAME array element (genuinely different from independently matching
different elements):

db.orders.find({
  items: { $elemMatch: { productId: 'p1', quantity: { $gte: 2 } } }
});

Projection - returning only specific fields:

db.products.find({ category: 'electronics' }, { name: 1, price: 1, _id: 0 });

sections.code:
// Sample order document:
// {
//   _id: ObjectId('...'),
//   customerId: 'c1',
//   items: [
//     { productId: 'p1', quantity: 1 },
//     { productId: 'p2', quantity: 5 }
//   ]
// }

// INCORRECT - intended to find orders where a SPECIFIC item (p1) was
// ordered in quantity >= 2, but this actually matches ANY order where
// SOME item is p1 AND SOME item (possibly different) has quantity >= 2
const incorrectResults = await ordersCollection.find({
  'items.productId': 'p1',
  'items.quantity': { $gte: 2 }
}).toArray();
// This WOULD incorrectly match the sample order above, even though
// p1 itself only has quantity 1, not >= 2

// CORRECT - using $elemMatch to require BOTH conditions on the SAME
// array element
const correctResults = await ordersCollection.find({
  items: {
    $elemMatch: { productId: 'p1', quantity: { $gte: 2 } }
  }
}).toArray();
// This correctly EXCLUDES the sample order above, since no single
// item has both productId 'p1' AND quantity >= 2 together

codingChallenge.starterCode:
// Sample cart that should NOT match (no single item has BOTH
// productId 'widget' AND quantity >= 3):
// {
//   items: [
//     { productId: 'widget', quantity: 1 },
//     { productId: 'gadget', quantity: 5 }
//   ]
// }

// BUGGY QUERY - identify why this incorrectly matches the sample cart:
const buggyQuery = {
  'items.productId': 'widget',
  'items.quantity': { $gte: 3 }
};

// TODO: explain the bug as a comment, then write the correct query
// using $elemMatch

codingChallenge.solutionExample:
// BUG EXPLANATION: This query independently checks 'some item has
// productId widget' (true, via the first item) and 'some item has
// quantity >= 3' (true, via the second item, gadget with quantity 5),
// without requiring both conditions to apply to the SAME item. The
// sample cart incorrectly matches even though widget's own quantity
// is only 1.

// CORRECT QUERY using $elemMatch:
const correctQuery = {
  items: {
    $elemMatch: { productId: 'widget', quantity: { $gte: 3 } }
  }
};

// This correctly excludes the sample cart, since no single item has
// both productId 'widget' AND quantity >= 3 together.

sections.code:
db.orders.aggregate([
  // Stage 1: only completed orders
  { $match: { status: 'completed' } },

  // Stage 2: calculate each order's total value (a derived field,
  // computed once, here, rather than recalculated repeatedly later)
  {
    $addFields: {
      orderTotal: { $sum: '$items.price' }
    }
  },

  // Stage 3: sort by that calculated total, descending
  { $sort: { customerId: 1, orderTotal: -1 } },

  // Stage 4: group by customer, collecting their orders into an array
  {
    $group: {
      _id: '$customerId',
      orders: { $push: { orderId: '$_id', total: '$orderTotal' } }
    }
  },

  // Stage 5: take only the first 3 from each customer's now-sorted
  // array of orders (their 3 highest-value orders, since sorting
  // already happened in stage 3 before grouping)
  {
    $project: {
      customerId: '$_id',
      topOrders: { $slice: ['$orders', 3] },
      _id: 0
    }
  }
]);

codingChallenge.starterCode:
// Sample reviews collection: { productId, category, rating }

// TODO: build a pipeline:
// 1. Group by category, calculating avgRating ($avg) and reviewCount ($sum: 1)
// 2. Filter ($match) to only categories with reviewCount >= 5
// 3. Sort by avgRating descending
// 4. Project the final shape: category, avgRating, reviewCount (no _id)

codingChallenge.solutionExample:
db.reviews.aggregate([
  {
    $group: {
      _id: '$category',
      avgRating: { $avg: '$rating' },
      reviewCount: { $sum: 1 }
    }
  },
  {
    $match: { reviewCount: { $gte: 5 } }
  },
  {
    $sort: { avgRating: -1 }
  },
  {
    $project: {
      category: '$_id',
      avgRating: 1,
      reviewCount: 1,
      _id: 0
    }
  }
]);

sections.content:
PostgreSQL, using BEGIN/COMMIT/ROLLBACK directly, or a client library's transaction helper:

const client = await pool.connect();
try {
  await client.query('BEGIN');
  await client.query('UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = $1', [fromId]);
  await client.query('UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = $1', [toId]);
  await client.query('COMMIT'); // both updates are now permanent, together
} catch (err) {
  await client.query('ROLLBACK'); // BOTH updates are undone, even though
                                     // the first one may have already run
  throw err;
} finally {
  client.release();
}

MongoDB, using a session-based transaction:

const session = client.startSession();
try {
  await session.withTransaction(async () => {
    await ordersCollection.insertOne({ customerId, items }, { session });
    await inventoryCollection.updateOne(
      { productId: items[0].productId },
      { $inc: { quantity: -items[0].quantity } },
      { session }
    );
  });
} finally {
  await session.endSession();
}

